  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is food technology a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Food technology is a real, growing branch tied to one of India's largest manufacturing sectors — not
    a dressed-up label with no substance behind it. But "is food technology good" and "will most
    graduates land the FMCG job the brochure promised" are two different questions, and most college
    admission pages blur them into one reassuring answer.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest split is this: the sector-level tailwind — export growth, government investment, rising
    processed-food demand — is genuinely real and not exaggerated. The entry-level reality for most
    individual graduates — modest starting pay, heavy documentation, and a smaller-than-expected FMCG
    hiring tier — is also genuinely real, and belongs to the specific person choosing this branch, not
    the industry as a whole.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "food industry never stops growing, join now" pitch flooding admission brochures,
      and it is not a blanket "never do food technology, it's a fallback trap" warning either. Both miss
      the real picture. The branch genuinely rewards people who go in with real interest and build a
      specialisation — and genuinely disappoints people who landed here only because the cutoff was low.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="reality-gap">The fallback-branch reality</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the part most "food technology scope" pages soften. The brochure version of this branch and
    the version a real incoming batch actually experiences are describing two different starting points,
    and that gap is the real source of a lot of graduate disappointment later.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the brochure shows</div>
      <ul>
        {realityGapComparison.gold.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the placement data and industry pattern actually show</div>
      <ul>
        {realityGapComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Neither side cancels the other out. Food technology is a genuinely lower-prestige, lower-starting-pay
      branch than mainstream engineering at most colleges, and it is also a real, specialisable, steadily
      growing career for people who actually want to do the work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Going in with accurate expectations about which of those two realities applies to you is what
      separates graduates who build a real career here from graduates who spend years feeling stuck in a
      branch they never actually wanted.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="admission-routes">BTech vs BSc: the real admission routes</h2>

  <p>
    "Food technology" and "food science" get used almost interchangeably in casual conversation, but the
    admission routes into them are genuinely different, and the difference matters for later eligibility,
    not just the entrance exam.
  </p>

  <div class="track-grid">
    {admissionRoutes.map((card) => (
      <div class="track-card">
        <h3>{card.title}</h3>
        <p>{card.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    None of these three routes is automatically "the right one." A BTech from a specialist institute
    like NIFTEM carries the strongest placement and PSU-eligibility profile; a BSc Food Science is a
    genuinely leaner, cheaper route into the same entry-level QA and regulatory roles; and a diploma is
    the fastest, lowest-cost path into plant and QC technician work specifically, with a lower ceiling
    unless it is followed by further study.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pay-ceiling">The real pay ceiling: is there genuine headroom, or is this role capped</h2>

  <p>
    "Food technology salary in India" is close to meaningless as a single number, because the spread
    between a fresher QA trainee and a senior plant quality head is large, and most admission marketing
    quotes only the flattering end of it.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
