        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ranges are directional, aggregated from 2025-2026 salary-tracking platforms including AmbitionBox,
    Glassdoor India, and Naukri, plus published FSSAI and state Food Safety Officer recruitment pay
    bands, at the time of writing. Verify current figures against live listings and recruitment
    notifications for your specific state, company, and role before anchoring a decision on any single
    number, including this one.
  </p>

  <p>
    So does food technology have real growth headroom, or is it structurally capped? The honest answer:
    it is capped early and slow-moving through the QA-generalist middle, but it is not capped at the top.
    A generalist who never specialises genuinely plateaus in the mid-career range above, meaningfully
    below what a core-engineering or IT peer from the same college earns at the same career stage.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ceiling that opens up — senior QA leadership, R&D-lead roles, or a food-safety-systems
    specialisation — belongs specifically to people who build a documented specialisation on top of the
    degree, not to tenure or the degree alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="career-lanes">The real career lanes in food technology</h2>

  <p>
    "Food technology" is not one job. It is five genuinely different work modes, each with its own daily
    reality, hiring volume, and pay ceiling. Comparing them honestly matters more than comparing colleges.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {careerLanes.map((item) => (
