        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    QA/QC inside FMCG and food manufacturing is, by hiring volume, the single largest lane for most
    graduates — not R&D, which is the lane students picture first but the one with the fewest openings.
    Matching your expectations to the actual hiring volume in each lane, before you graduate, avoids a
    lot of wasted job-search months.
  </p>

  <p>
    None of these five lanes pay meaningfully more than the others at entry level. The income gap opens
    up later, and it opens up specifically for people who turn one lane into a documented
    <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> — a real HACCP or ISO 22000 audit trail, a shipped
    reformulation project, or a packaging-shelf-life study — instead of staying a generalist across all
    five indefinitely.
  </p>

  <h2 id="market-tailwind">The processed-food tailwind that is genuinely real</h2>

  <p>
    This is the one part of the "food technology is a good career" pitch that is not exaggerated. India's
    processed-food sector is a real, large, and fast-growing part of the economy, and that growth is a
    genuine tailwind for long-term hiring demand — it just does not automatically translate into a higher
    starting salary for every fresher.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
