  <p>
    The honest way to read this data: it explains why QA, regulatory, and plant-operations roles keep
    opening up across hundreds of manufacturers year after year, not why a fresh graduate's first payslip
    should be large. Sector-level growth funds job creation at scale; it does not fund a premium
    entry-level salary the way a high-demand, talent-scarce tech role does.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-risk">Will automation change food technology jobs</h2>

  <p>
    This is the question underneath every "is food technology a good career" search once someone gets
    past the salary numbers. The honest answer is not a flat yes or no — it depends on which half of the
    job you actually do.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is already being automated</div>
      <ul>
        {aiComparison.gold.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What still needs a human</div>
      <ul>
        {aiComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Plant automation and digital quality-management systems are spreading fast across mid-size and
      large Indian food manufacturers, and the pressure lands almost entirely on routine, scriptable
      checks: inline sensing, defect detection, and batch-record logging. It does not land on sensory
      judgment, root-cause investigation, or regulator-facing decisions — the exact work that still needs
      a person who can read the situation and take responsibility for the call.
    </p>
    <p>
      The safer response is pairing core food-science knowledge with digital quality-systems fluency —
      knowing how to work with, check, and improve an automated QA workflow — rather than avoiding the
      field or ignoring the shift entirely.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice this is a staged move, not a one-time switch: start by learning which checks on your
      line are already automated so you are not blindsided by a sensor quietly doing your old job, then
      get comfortable auditing what the automated system flags, and only later move toward owning how
      the digital quality workflow itself is designed. Each stage is realistic to build on the job — none
      of it requires becoming a software engineer.
    </p>
  </div>

